  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and warm conditions through Saturday. Colder \ntemperatures expected early next week.\n\nA broad area of surface high pressure remains overhead for much of \nthe eastern US through Saturday, bringing dry conditions and calm \nwinds, as well as above normal temperatures. Afternoon high \ntemperatures will climb to the 60s to near 70 today, which is about \n10 to 15 degrees above normal. Clear skies and light winds during \nthe overnight will keep lows in the 20s and 30s. Lows will gradually \nstart to warm as low level moisture begins to advect into the region \nby Sunday.\n\nFollowing a frontal passage this weekend, cold high pressure returns \nto the area, and will bring a drop in temperatures for early next \nweek. High temperatures in the 60s are expected for Sunday, but then \nfall into the 40s and 50s by next Tuesday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Chances for rain this weekend, but forecast \nuncertainty remains in amounts. Precipitation chances increase \nfor Thanksgiving.\n\nA coastal low pressure system is forecast to develop off the \nsoutheast coast late in the work week, and then track northward \nalong the Atlantic seaboard through the weekend. There is still \nconsiderable uncertainty in the track of this low, as guidance \ncontinues to vary among models and from run to run. There has been \nsome consistency that the low initially will linger off the FL/GA \ncoast before getting absorbed into the broader 500mb flow as a \nstrong trough approaches from the west and then track \nnorth/northeastward. Most of the precipitation at this time looks to \nbe confined to eastern VA and NC, with only light rain in the \nSouthside. However, if the low tracks farther west, could see higher \nrainfall amounts and rain closer to the Foothills. Any rain in this \nregion would be beneficial, given the persistent Severe to Extreme \ndrought conditions across southern VA and eastern NC. NBM \nprobabilities for 24 hour rainfall through Sunday evening of at \nleast half an inch of rain are 25% for the Foothills and Piedmont, \nand less than 20% from the I-81 corridor and farther west. That \nsaid, there is considerable spread in rainfall amounts, as the 90th \npercentile for places along and east of Danville, VA are 2+ inches. \n\nThe upper trough will bring a cold front through the area late in \nthe weekend as well, with strong high pressure following closely \nbehind. Low rainfall chances for the mountains come with this \nfrontal passage, but with the better upper level support farther to \nthe north, not expecting much precipitation from this. The high \npressure will bring dry and brisk conditions for the first half of \nnext week.\n\nLooking ahead to Thanksgiving, the surface high builds across \nthe northeastern US, with a cold air damming scenario developing\nfor the Appalachians towards the middle of next week. Long \nrange deterministic guidance shows another 500mb trough \ndeepening east of the Rockies then tracking east, as well as \nanother potential coastal system forming near the southeast \nmidweek. Impacts to the local area are uncertain at this time, \nbut is the next system to watch, especially as the timing\ncoincides with major travel days.",
